# Architecture & Code Organization (Angular)

For the full View module layer model, tier patterns, folder layout, naming conventions, and examples, see `.cursor/docs/frontend/angular-module-architecture.md`.

## Pattern Reuse

- When creating a new store, service, or resource, examine sibling Views with the same suffix to match folder placement and API contract
- When adding to an extensible pattern (port tokens, resources, stores), name and scope new artifacts at the same category level as siblings, not narrowly for a single use case
- Prefer reusing existing patterns and registration points (`InjectionToken` ports, route `providers`, store command methods) over inventing new ones. Check how similar Views are already wired before creating new infrastructure

## Module Layering

- Before adding code to a shared service or component, verify the layering by checking existing imports. Ensure the change respects the dependency direction: `ui → store → infrastructure → domain → api`
- `ui/` must not import `dto/` or `HttpClient` directly — go through the store and its resource port
- Declare port interfaces and their `InjectionToken`s in `api/`; implement them in `infrastructure/resources/`
- Bind ports in the route's `providers` array (or the View component's `providers`), never inside the component that consumes them

## Store Responsibility

- Do not add new methods to an existing store based on a superficial name match. Read the store's existing signals and callers to verify its actual responsibility
- Do not couple UI components to an implementation detail (e.g. request URL, mock flag) when a stable port (`FOO_RESOURCE`) exists
- Expose readonly signals to the UI; change state only through explicit command methods on the store

## Angular Idioms

- Components are standalone — declare dependencies in `imports`, never create an `NgModule`
- Use `inject()` rather than constructor parameter injection in new code
- Set `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` on new components
- The app is zoneless (`zone.js` is not installed) — hold component and store state in signals; mutating plain fields will not reliably update the view
- Use `input()` / `output()` signal APIs, not the `@Input()` / `@Output()` decorators
- Use built-in control flow (`@if`, `@for` with `track`, `@switch`) in templates, not `*ngIf` / `*ngFor`
- Prefer signals (`signal`, `computed`, `resource`) over `BehaviorSubject`. Use RxJS for event streams, debouncing, and cancellation, then bridge with `toSignal()`
- Clean up subscriptions with `takeUntilDestroyed()` or the async pipe
- Provide View-scoped services on the View component or route; reserve `providedIn: 'root'` for genuinely app-wide services
- Navigate with `routerLink` / `Router.navigate` — never `window.location`
- No side effects in getters or template expressions; use event handlers, `effect()`, or store methods

## Styling

- Use Tailwind utility classes. Component `.css` files are for cases utilities cannot cover
- Watch the per-component style budget in `angular.json` (4 kB warning / 8 kB error)
- Global styles belong in `src/styles.css` only when they cannot live in component classes
